# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
/sensei-code
/bin/

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# Sensei Code local, non-authoritative state
/.sensei-code/

# Sensei local Oxigraph store data.
/.sensei/data/
# Per-checkout runtime proof artifacts, rewritten by every `sensei build` and by
# some reads. They describe the state of one machine's live store — the closure
# report even records an absolute source root — so they are runtime truth about
# a process, not source about a revision. Tracking them produced a diff on every
# build, would conflict on any branch, and dirtied the canonical checkout badly
# enough to trip the governed dirty-tree gate. Worse, a branch checkout could
# resurrect a stale proof and present it as current. The authored corpus is
# docs/awareness/; .sensei/config.yaml, .sensei/project/ and .sensei/skills/
# stay tracked. The sibling services repository ignores the same three files.
/.sensei/graph-authority.json
/.sensei/graph-authority.transaction.tsv
/.sensei/graph-closure-report.json

# Derived project inference, and the one file in .sensei/project/ that cannot be
# tracked honestly. Its binding names an exact revision, so committing it makes
# it wrong the moment anything else is committed: the artifact would claim to
# describe a commit that no longer exists in the branch's tip, and every
# receipt built from it would inherit that claim. Keeping it current in git
# would mean regenerating 13 MB on every commit. It is regenerable from the
# revision it names, and must be regenerated from a CLEAN tree — generating it
# from a dirty one binds working-tree facts to HEAD and reports them as
# resolved (globulario/sensei#216).
/.sensei/project/claims.yaml

# Sensei task execution state: workspaces, checkpoints, append-only ledgers,
# candidate history. Durable, and durable under Sensei's own contract rather
# than under this repository's source history.
#
# The two authorities must not be the same one. A governed run writes task
# evidence into the tree it is governing; if that evidence is repository state,
# the run dirties its own checkout, and the base a candidate is established from
# moves because the machinery observed itself. That is the self-reference this
# project removes everywhere else, and it would arrive here as a convenience.
#
# Contents mattering is not the question. A write-ahead log is evidence that
# something happened and is still not source code, and committing a probe task's
# ninety-file workspace would make an experiment's operational exhaust part of
# the product tree because the experiment happened.
#
# The rule, if repository-visible provenance is wanted later: records may be
# durable without being repository state, and promotion into Git must itself be
# an explicit governance action. Promote a small immutable receipt under a
# dedicated evidence path — task id, base revision, judged revision,
# disposition, verification digest, ledger digest — never the workspace.
/.sensei/tasks/
